def rule_layer_membership_is_declared_not_inferred_from_a_filename_prefix(sample_text, ports):
    """Membership of a layer is a RELATION — the executable imports the layer's package, or cites the layer's interface document — and it must be computed from that relation, never from how the file happens to be named. A naming convention standing in for a boundary silently shrinks the population every time the layer grows in a direction the convention did not anticipate, and the shrinkage is invisible because the members that fell out were never counted."""
    # Expected signal: ERROR
    # Suggested fix action: Define the population as every executable that imports the layer's package or cites the layer's interface document, and take the prefix glob as one contributor rather than as the definition. Assert that the relation-derived population is a superset of the glob-derived one, and drive every arm of the layer property over the relation-derived set. NOTE ON WHERE THIS LIVES: same home as the population-equality rule, and they share a helper — the relation-derived population is the input both need, so computing it twice is how the two answers start to disagree. CORPUS-SWEPT, AND IT NEEDS A DISCRIMINATOR THE RECORD DID NOT HAVE: 32 test files select a population by filename prefix, and MOST ARE CORRECT — the universal prefix that means `every test` is exactly the right selector for a suite-wide property. The rule fires only where the prefix stands in for a SEMANTIC SUBSET, a layer or a family, because that is where the naming convention and the boundary can drift apart. Distinguish them by whether the prefix names a subset at all: the universal one has no sibling population it could be missing. AND THE COST OF BEING OUTSIDE THE POPULATION IS A WRONG ANSWER, NOT A MISSING ONE. The one false vacuous reading on record was taken against a program the prefix does not reach: its input is a document and it was handed an empty directory, so it answered correctly about a population nobody had asked it about. Members outside the boundary do not sit un-examined; they get examined by improvised arms, which is worse. A THIRD MEASURED INSTANCE, AND IT RESOLVES A QUESTION A LANE LEFT OPEN. Two commands of one layer answer the same question — a readable input that yields nothing — with different codes: one returns 0 and the other returns 2. A lane reported the disagreement and said it could not establish from the code which was the layer's answer. The layer HAS an answer: its own present-but-empty test asserts 2, three times. The reason nobody could see it is that the answer lives in a test whose population is the prefix, and the command returning 0 is one of the executables the prefix misses. So the boundary does not merely leave members unchecked; it hides the settled answer from the people who own them. RE-MEASURED after merging 30 commits of main: the six rc codes are unchanged (2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 3) and a flag that exists still returns 0, so the finding is current rather than carried forward. The two offenders now have a named home: main landed `programs/_gate_usage_exit.py`, which exists because rc 2 already means VACUOUS and a bad invocation therefore needs rc 3. The 'one-line shared fix' this record asks for is that module, so the implementing lane imports it rather than writing the constant again.
